---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const m = '/media/cafe';
---

<Base
  title="Café Racer Concept"
  description="A complete motorcycle modelled in SolidWorks, a frame redesigned in ANSYS from FoS 0.27 to 1.14, two parts planned for manufacture, and a product film rendered in Blender."
  sheet="2 of 3"
  dwg="MDR-002"
>
  <header class="cs-head wrap">
    <div class="cs-head__top label">
      <a href="/#work">← All work</a>
      <span>Independent project · 2026</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Café Racer <em>Concept</em></h1>
    <p class="cs-head__lede">
      I rebuilt a complete motorcycle in SolidWorks from a training model, then treated it as if it had to be built and
      ridden: found its loads, analysed the frame and gearbox, fixed what failed, and planned how two key parts would be
      made.
    </p>

    <dl class="spec">
      <div><dt class="label">CAD</dt><dd>217 parts, 41 assemblies, 42 drawings</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="label">Frame</dt><dd>FoS 0.27 → 1.14</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="label">Output shaft</dt><dd>Fatigue FoS 1.74, infinite life</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="label">Tools</dt><dd>SolidWorks · ANSYS · Blender</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <div class="cs-media">
      <video controls preload="none" playsinline poster={`${m}/hero.webp`}>
        <source src="/media/cafe-racer-film.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
      </video>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="chapter" style="border-top: 0;">
      <div class="chapter__side label"><b>Overview</b>The vehicle</div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <h2 class="h2">The whole bike, <em>part by part.</em></h2>
        <div class="pair">
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              Every part was modelled in SolidWorks and built up through sub-assemblies into one top-level assembly. The
              bodywork (tank, seat, side and rear panels) is my own design.
            </p>
            <p>
              The CAD engine weighed only 18.4 kg: thin walls, a hollow flywheel, no starter. I set it to 36 kg for the
              load cases, because a light engine would have hidden the real frame loads.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="table-scroll">
            <table class="data">
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Engine</td><td class="num">Parallel twin, Ø60 bore, 250 cc class</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Gearbox</td><td class="num">5-speed · primary 1.571 · 1st 2.00</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Wheelbase / head angle</td><td class="num">1403.54 mm / 33.0°</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Seat height</td><td class="num">740 mm</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Mass</td><td class="num">151.75 kg dry (CAD) · 162 kg wet</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Front / rear split</td><td class="num">40.4 / 59.6% with two riders</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figure class="fig fig--render reveal">
          <img src={`${m}/exploded.webp`} alt="Exploded view: bodywork lifted off the trellis frame, engine dropped below, front and rear ends pulled apart." loading="lazy" />
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter">
      <div class="chapter__side label"><b>A</b>Frame</div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <h2 class="h2">The frame failed. <em>Thicker</em> tube didn't fix it.</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <h3>Load case</h3>
          <p>
            A hard bump briefly triples every weight on the bike. I stacked that on a rider and a passenger (312 kg in
            total) so the frame is sized for the worst day, not an average one. Loads go in where parts bolt on: the
            engine into its four mounts, the riders onto the seat rails, and the suspension forces onto the shock tab
            and linkage bracket, each solved by hand through the rising-rate linkage.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rear wheel carries 5469.9 N at 3 g, but the shock pushes on the frame with 23 215 N: 7.6 times the
            weight of the whole loaded bike.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pair reveal">
          <figure class="fig">
            <img src={`${m}/before_stress.webp`} alt="ANSYS beam stress plot of the original frame, peak near the shock mount." loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>Before: 1692.6 MPa in the Ø12 solid braces between the shock mount and swingarm pivot. FoS 0.27.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="fig">
            <img src={`${m}/final_stress.webp`} alt="ANSYS beam stress plot of the redesigned frame with a much lower, more evenly spread peak." loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>After: 404.0 MPa, shared between one seat-brace joint and the shock-mount curve. FoS 1.14. Outside shape unchanged.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <p class="pull reveal">The problem was how the load <em>entered</em> the frame, not how thick that one tube was.</p>

        <div class="prose">
          <h3>Why it failed</h3>
          <p>
            The shock pushes the frame up with 23 kN while the swingarm pivot, only 244 mm below, pulls it down. In the
            original frame that path ran through Ø12 solid bars loaded in bending. Even standing still with two riders
            it sat at 564 MPa, above yield.
          </p>
          <h3>What I changed</h3>
          <p>
            AISI 4130 normalised tube, yield 460 MPa. I changed only the tubes on that load path: shock-area curves,
            down tube and lower diagonals to Ø35×30, and the two highest-loaded members to a 4 mm wall. My first
            instinct, upsizing the tube with the peak, did nothing (661.5 → 675.9 MPa).
          </p>
          <h3>Result</h3>
          <p>
            FoS 0.27 → 1.14 in the worst case. Tail deflection 59.8 → 8.6 mm. Frame mass 18.1 → 20.5 kg (+13%) for 4.2×
            lower peak stress. This is a static check only; weld fatigue and heat-affected-zone strength are next.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="fig reveal">
          <img src={`${m}/fig_redesign2.webp`} alt="Bar chart of peak frame stress over ten design iterations against the 460 MPa yield line." loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>
            Peak stress per iteration. Phase 1 fixed the load path. The swingarm study then measured the link lever arm
            at 119.28 mm, not 159 mm: the link load rose 33%, the frame failed again (550.5 MPa), and four targeted
            changes adding about 1.4 kg restored FoS 1.14.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="prose">
          <h3>How I knew the model was right</h3>
          <p>
            A free-free modal run gave exactly six rigid-body modes, so every tube was connected. The steering-head
            reaction landed 2.0% from my hand calculation. Halving the mesh size changed the peak by only 3.2%.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter">
      <div class="chapter__side label"><b>B</b>Swingarm</div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <h2 class="h2">A sideways hit governs, <em>not</em> the chain.</h2>
        <div class="pair">
          <figure class="fig">
            <img src={`${m}/sw_render.webp`} alt="Render of the swingarm: two box-section arms joined by a solid crossmember." loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>AISI 4130 normalised, 7.659 kg. Two 18 × 60 × 2 mm box arms joined by a solid 50 mm crossmember that carries the shock-link lugs.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="fig">
            <img src={`${m}/sw_corner_stress.webp`} alt="ANSYS stress plot of the swingarm under the lateral load case." loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>Lateral envelope: 434.1 MPa at the arm-to-block joint, FoS 1.06. The governing case.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="table-scroll reveal">
          <table class="data">
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Load case</th><th scope="col">Peak stress</th><th scope="col">FoS</th><th scope="col">Deflection</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>3 g vertical bump</td><td class="num">295.6 MPa</td><td class="num">1.56</td><td class="num">3.59 mm</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Chain pull 2218 N, one side</td><td class="num">36.1 MPa</td><td class="num">12.7</td><td class="num">0.35 mm</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Steady cornering, 45° lean (scaled from the bump run)</td><td class="num">≈ 139 MPa</td><td class="num">≈ 3.3</td><td class="num">≈ 1.7 mm</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Lateral envelope: 1 g vertical + 1 g sideways</td><td class="num">434.1 MPa</td><td class="delta">1.06</td><td class="num">6.45 mm</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <h3>Reading it</h3>
          <p>
            The arm is 60 mm tall but only 18 mm wide, so it is 7.3× stiffer and 2.2× stronger up-and-down than
            sideways. A genuine sideways load (a crossed-up landing, a kerb strike, a sliding tyre suddenly gripping)
            finds that weak axis.
          </p>
          <p>
            My hand calculation had flagged the chain pull as the worst case at 311 MPa, treating it as sideways
            bending. The FEA showed the chain pulls along the arm, so the arm carries it mostly in tension: 36 MPa.
          </p>
          <h3>Lightweighting, in progress</h3>
          <p>
            The 4.90 kg solid crossmember sits mostly below 70 MPa while the arm walls next to it carry 300. A topology
            run at 35% retained mass empties the core and keeps material on diagonal paths from the lug bosses to each
            arm joint. Swingarms are welded from tube and plate, so I'm using that result as a map of load paths, not a
            part: a round cross-tube with gussets is being modelled now.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pair reveal">
          <figure class="fig">
            <img src={`${m}/sw_joint.webp`} alt="Stress plot showing the solid crossmember lightly loaded next to highly stressed arm walls." loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>Baseline, bump case: the solid block sits mostly below 70 MPa.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="fig">
            <img src={`${m}/sw_topo.webp`} alt="Topology optimisation result with the core of the crossmember removed." loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>Topology result, 35% retained: material stays on diagonal paths from the lugs to each arm joint.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter">
      <div class="chapter__side label"><b>C</b>Transmission</div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <h2 class="h2">The shaft passes fatigue. The gear teeth <em>don't.</em></h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <h3>Following the torque</h3>
          <p>
            24 N·m at the crank × 66/42 in the primary drive × 52/26 in 1st gear = 75.4 N·m on the output shaft: 3.1
            times the engine's torque, on a neck only 16 mm across. That neck is the thinnest section, carries the full
            torque, and sits beside bearing A where the sprocket's overhang moment peaks. By hand: 183.5 MPa von Mises,
            FoS 3.0. The FEA later agreed on the location.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="fig reveal">
          <img src={`${m}/fig_fbd.webp`} alt="Free-body diagram of the output shaft in first gear with bearing reactions, gear forces and chain pull." loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Free-body diagram, 1st gear. Worst case: chain pull opposite the gear resultant. The critical section is the Ø16 neck beside bearing A.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="pair reveal">
          <figure class="fig">
            <img src={`${m}/shaft_singular.webp`} alt="Stress plot with a sharp peak at the spline-end corner of the shaft." loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>Rejected peak: the spline-end corner rose 270.7 → 343.2 → 491.7 MPa with refinement. A singularity next to the loads, so it was checked by hand instead (FoS 3.4).</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="fig">
            <img src={`${m}/fig_goodman.webp`} alt="Goodman diagram for the shaft neck showing the operating point inside the infinite-life line." loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>Goodman diagram, Ø16 neck. Se = 268.6 MPa after Marin factors, Kf 1.10 → n = 1.74 for infinite life.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <h3>Conclusion</h3>
          <p>
            The shaft is fine; the teeth are the weak link. The current 1st-gear pair (module 1.1, 8 mm face) reaches
            1511 MPa in bending against a 379 MPa limit. A resized pair (module 2.5, 16 mm face) passes bending at FoS
            1.28 but is still marginal in pitting, so it needs about a 20 mm face. Resizing moves both shafts and the
            cases, so it is logged as future work.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter">
      <div class="chapter__side label"><b>D</b>Manufacture</div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <h2 class="h2">Two parts taken to a <em>making</em> plan.</h2>

        <div class="pair">
          <div class="prose">
            <h3>Output shaft: bar to ground journal</h3>
            <p>
              Every operation holds the shaft between the same two centre holes, so all diameters stay concentric.
              Carburizing distorts the shaft slightly, so the bearing seats are left 0.2 mm big and ground to size
              afterwards. The spline-end singularity from the FEA became a drawing note: R0.5 minimum run-out, which
              the gear-shaping cutter leaves for free.
            </p>
          </div>
          <ol class="steps steps--ops">
            <li><b>Op 10–20</b><span>Cut 8620 bar Ø22 × 155; face, centre-drill</span></li>
            <li><b>Op 30</b><span>CNC turn between centres, seats +0.2 mm</span></li>
            <li><b>Op 40</b><span>Hob 12-spline; shape 8-spline</span></li>
            <li><b>Op 50</b><span>Carburize 0.5–0.8 mm, 58–62 HRC</span></li>
            <li><b>Op 60–70</b><span>Grind Ø20 k5, Ra 0.4; inspect</span></li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <figure class="fig reveal">
          <img src={`${m}/shaft_dwg-1.webp`} alt="Dimensioned engineering drawing of the output shaft." loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Output shaft drawing.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="prose">
          <h3>Right engine case: A356-T6 permanent-mould casting</h3>
          <p>
            The model had been drawn like a machined part: no draft, 3 mm walls meeting Ø10 bosses, R1 inside corners
            and an undercut saddle that needs a side core. The review produced seven fixes, from 1°/2° draft and cored
            bosses to a side slide for the saddle.
          </p>
          <p>
            It also caught a part that could not be bought. The gearbox bearing seats were drawn at Ø36, but no
            catalogue bearing for a Ø20 shaft is 36 mm outside. The nearest real part is a 6904 (20 × 37 × 9), so the
            seats became Ø37 N7. By 100 °C the aluminium seat opens by 0.029 mm, enough to wipe out almost all of the
            interference, so the seats get retainer plates.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pair reveal">
          <figure class="fig">
            <img src={`${m}/case_annotated.webp`} alt="Annotated view of the right engine case marking casting problems." loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>Design-for-casting review of the right engine case.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="fig">
            <img src={`${m}/case_drawing-1.webp`} alt="Engineering drawing of the right engine case." loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>Right engine case drawing.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter">
      <div class="chapter__side label"><b>E</b>Film</div>
      <div class="chapter__body">
        <h2 class="h2">From assembly tree to <em>seventy</em> seconds.</h2>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li><b>CAD</b><span>Every part modelled in SolidWorks 2026 and built up through sub-assemblies.</span></li>
          <li><b>Export</b><span>A VBA macro walks the assembly tree and exports each sub-assembly to glTF, so the Blender scene keeps the CAD structure.</span></li>
          <li><b>Rendering</b><span>Materials, lighting and animation in Blender 5.2 with Cycles. 1,620 frames at 2560 × 1440 on a render farm.</span></li>
          <li><b>Edit</b><span>Captions and the end card are added by a Python script in Blender's sequencer.</span></li>
          <li><b>Sound</b><span>A second script builds the music edit, engine and synthesized effects and masters the mix.</span></li>
        </ol>

        <div class="trio reveal">
          <figure class="fig fig--render"><img src={`${m}/hero.webp`} alt="The café racer in powder blue with orange wheels." loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <figure class="fig fig--render"><img src={`${m}/finish-green.webp`} alt="The café racer in the Army Green finish." loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <figure class="fig fig--render"><img src={`${m}/finish-black.webp`} alt="The café racer in the Midnight Black finish." loading="lazy" /></figure>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <h3>Credits</h3>
          <p>
            The project started from the part reference drawings in Christopher Rothera's Udemy course "PTC Creo
            Parametric - Full Motorbike Build", which I remodelled in SolidWorks. The bodywork is my own design, the
            frame was redesigned to pass structural analysis, and a number of other parts were modified along the way.
            Music: "Hard EDM" by kulakovka, from Pixabay. Studio lighting from Poly Haven; several materials from
            BlenderKit.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/brickflows/motorcycle">CAD, scripts and drawings on GitHub <span class="arrow">→</span></a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <a class="next" href="/work/front-wing/">
      <span class="label">Next project</span>
      <span>Front wing in ground effect <span class="arrow">→</span></span>
    </a>
  </div>
</Base>
